      "type": "performancesAndVision",
      "text": "Griffin Dunne delivers a perfectly calibrated performance as Paul, capturing the transformation from smug yuppie to desperate survivor with increasing hysteria that never crosses into caricature. Scorsese's direction marks a radical departure from his previous work, demonstrating his versatility by crafting a paranoid comedy that channels both German Expressionism and film noir. The film shares DNA with **Taxi Driver** (1976) in its nocturnal New York setting, but replaces violence with absurdist humor. Michael Ballhaus's cinematography creates a disorienting dreamscape through canted angles and fluid camera movements, though occasionally the film's technical ambition exceeds its modest budget constraints. The supporting cast, including Rosanna Arquette and Catherine O'Hara, populate this nightmare with memorably unhinged characters."
    },
    {
      "type": "socialCulturalAndRelevance",
      "text": "After Hours captures a specific moment in New York City's history, when SoHo straddled the line between artistic bohemia and gentrification. The film's themes of class anxiety and urban alienation echo similar concerns in **American Psycho** (2000) and **Lost in Translation** (2003), though with a distinctly 1980s perspective on yuppie culture. It prefigures the 'one crazy night' structure later seen in films like **Go** (1999), but with a darker, more surreal edge. The film's depiction of a man lost in an increasingly hostile urban landscape resonates even more strongly in today's world of digital disconnection and urban anonymity. While some of the film's gender politics feel dated, its exploration of class divisions and urban tribalism remains relevant. The pacing occasionally meanders in its middle section, but this actually enhances the nightmare logic of Paul's journey."
